course: dictionary
lesson: 13476
title: Hypnotised and the words around it
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them show which one you need. «The swinging watch hypnotised the sleepy man.» The watch swings slowly and the man drifts into a sleep-like state. Hypnotised means put into a very relaxed state, so the clues point to that meaning.
explain: «The children sat hypnotised by the cartoon, and nobody heard the bell.» Nobody heard the bell because the children could not stop watching. This is the second meaning: so fascinated by something that you cannot stop watching it. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence to check it.
explain: Hypnotised is the past form of hypnotise. Look for the phrases «hypnotised by» and «as if hypnotised», because they often show the second meaning. When the clues are not enough, a dictionary will settle it.
fact: Hypnotise is a verb, and hypnotised is its past form.
fact: «Hypnotised» can also be an adjective, describing someone who is in this state.
fact: «Hypnotised» means put into a sleep-like state in which a person is very relaxed and open to suggestions.
fact: «Hypnotised» also means so fascinated by something that you cannot stop watching it.
fact: Common phrases are «hypnotised by» and «as if hypnotised».
exercise: «The swinging watch hypnotised the sleepy man.» What does hypnotised mean here? | *Put into a sleep-like, very relaxed state | Woken up by a loud noise | Angry and shouting at the watch | Lost in a busy street
exercise: «Sam was hypnotised by the screen and did not hear his name.» What does hypnotised mean here? | Asleep in his bed | *So fascinated that he could not stop watching | Bored and ready to leave | Cold and wet from the rain
exercise: Find and copy one word that is the past form of hypnotise. | *hypnotised | hypnotising | hypnotises | hypnosis
test: «The magician's voice made the crowd feel as if hypnotised.» What does this suggest about the crowd? | They were angry at the magician | They were hungry for lunch | They were cold and wet | *They were so absorbed they seemed to be under a spell
test: «The baby was hypnotised by the swinging mobile above her cot.» What does hypnotised mean here? | *Unable to stop watching the moving mobile | Running around the room | Frightened of the dark | Hungry for her milk
exam: «The audience sat hypnotised, unable to look away from the stage.» What does hypnotised mean here? | Angry about the show | *So fascinated that they could not stop watching | Bored and falling asleep in their chairs | Excited and running to the exit
